**Code Explanation:**

The code generates a 2x3 grid of subplots to visualize the spatial statistics and model deviations computed in the previous steps. It loads the aggregated data from the .npz files saved in the data/ directory. 1. Satellite Radial Profiles: Plots the empirical mean density of satellites as a function of normalized distance r/R_vir for three halo mass bins, with error bars representing the standard deviation across the 10 realizations. A theoretical slope proportional to exp(-r/R_vir) is overlaid for comparison. 2. KS Test D-statistics: Displays a bar chart of the Kolmogorov-Smirnov D-statistics for each realization, comparing the empirical satellite radial distribution to the theoretical CDF. A dashed line indicates the mean across all realizations. 3. Global VPF: Plots the empirical and theoretical (Monte Carlo) Void Probability Functions P0(r) on a logarithmic scale. A secondary y-axis shows the residual VPF (Empirical - Theoretical) with error bars. 4. Residual VPF by Mass Bin: Decomposes the residual VPF into contributions from different halo mass bins, highlighting mass-dependent deviations from the theoretical model. 5. Marked Correlation Function: Plots the luminosity-weighted marked correlation function M(r) in the 1-halo regime (r < 5 Mpc/h). A baseline at M(r)=1 is included to indicate the unmarked expectation. 6. 2PCF in Transition Regime: Compares the empirical 2-Point Correlation Function xi(r) against a decoupled model (where satellite positions are shuffled across halos of similar mass) in the 5-10 Mpc/h transition regime. The figure is saved as a high-resolution PNG file in the data/ directory, and key summary statistics are printed to the console for the researcher's reference.

